to prove they were right.", "reference": "2012, Kevin Neary, Leigh A. Tobin, Major League Dads:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}]}, {"definition": "(transitive, Australia) To injure through a blow; to induce a haematoma.", "tags": ["Australia", "transitive"], "examples": ["The vicious tackle corked his leg.", "2010, Andrew Stojanovski, Dog Ear Cafe, large print 16pt, page 191,\nMuch to my relief he had only corked his leg when he had jumped."], "quotes": [{"text": "Injuries, which seemed to be of an inconsequential nature, were often sustained, such as a sprained ankle, a dislocated phalanx, a twisted foot, a corked leg and so on.", "reference": "2006, Joseph N. Santamaria, The Education of Dr Joe, page 60:"}, {"text": "As he moved away again, William winced at an ache in his thigh.\n‘Must have corked my leg when I got up,’ he thought.", "reference": "2007, Shaun A. Saunders, Navigating in the New World, page 202:"}, {"text": "“I′m okay. I must have corked my thigh when Bruce fell onto me. I′ll be fine.”", "reference": "2008, Christopher J. Holcroft, Canyon, page 93:"}, {"text": "I corked my thigh late in the game, which we won, and came off.", "reference": "2010, Ben Cousins, Ben Cousins: My Life Story, page 108:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "aiheuttaa mustelma"}, {"language": {"code": "fi", "name": "Finnish"}, "word": "korkata"}], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(fishing) To position one's drift net just outside of another person's net, thereby intercepting and catching all the fish that would have gone into that person's net.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Kate remembered then, the family fish camp a mile or so up Amartuq Creek, the very creek across the mouth of which Yuri Andreev had tried to cork Joe Anahonak not half an hour before.", "reference": "1998, Dana Stabenow, Killing Grounds, →ISBN, page 8:"}, {"text": "But its soon apparent that there are more boats than fish—at least for the moment. We all drift quietly, keeping an eye out for other boats and other nets. Corking another guy's net is a screaming—bastard offense.", "reference": "2003, George Lowe, Fisherman: The Strife and Times of Ronald K. Peterson of Ballard, →ISBN:"}, {"text": "You're pissed if someone sets too close to you and especially if he sets his net right along yours, \"corking\" you and intercepting the fish that seem headed to your own net. I was close to this guy's outside net, but definitely not corking him.", "reference": "2008, Bert Bender, Catching the Ebb: Drift-fishing for a Life in Cook Inlet, →ISBN, page 249:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive) To block (a street) illegally, to allow a protest or other activity to take place without traffic.", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "[…] corking the streets is a challenge to capitalist ideologies, like skateboarding in parking lots and walkways […]", "reference": "2022, Victoria A. Newsom, Lara Martin Lengel, Embodied Activisms, page 70:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/kɔɹk/", "tags": ["General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/kɔːk/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}], "forms": [{"word": "corks", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(snowboarding, skiing, skateboarding) An aerialist maneuver involving a rotation where the rider goes heels over head, with the board overhead.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [{"language": {"code": "fr", "name": "French"}, "word": "cork"}], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "p